 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 
-Mechanistic accounts of grokking rest on two progress measures: the set of Fourier frequencies a circuit occupies and the fraction of representational power they carry \citep{nanda2023progress}. Together they are the standard certificate that a circuit is present. We exhibit a regime in which the certificate and the function come apart. At the step a grokked transformer stops computing modular addition, the frequency support of the family that computes the task is identical to its pre-collapse state and its power distribution retains a cosine similarity of $0.9899$ (Figure~\ref{fig:collapse}). Both measures are invariant under a change of basis of the residual stream: support under any invertible map, and power under any orthogonal one. The failure we study moves along this freedom: the spectral certificate is unchanged while the compatibility between representation and readout fails, and measures of the function register it in full.
+Mechanistic accounts of grokking rest on two progress measures: the set of Fourier frequencies a circuit occupies and the fraction of representational power they carry \citep{nanda2023progress}. Together they are the standard certificate that a circuit is present. We exhibit a regime in which the certificate and the function come apart. At the step a grokked transformer stops computing modular addition, the dominant-frequency membership of the task-aligned family is unchanged and its power distribution retains a cosine similarity of $0.9899$ (Figure~\ref{fig:collapse}). This spectral stability raises a natural hypothesis: the representation has changed basis while the readout has failed to compensate. We test that hypothesis directly. Fresh decoders recover the task, but measured residuals fail exact global basis-equivalence tests. Preserved task information and preserved spectral statistics therefore require separate tests of the mechanism.
 
